#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087464 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "19d1448c-d5fd-5615-b657-ff4d66867834" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Wide Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "67da2ffa-b472-5084-985c-dde4cd2df661" :question/math-academy-id "e-5219" :question/problem "What is the equation of the ellipse given below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/849/Source/Images/89500561af6affb81c697e1bffe36957.png)" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{{a}^2}+\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{{b}^2}=1\n$$\n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(1,-1)$, the horizontal radius has length $a=5$, and the vertical radius has length $b=3$. Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((x - h)^{2})/(a^{2}) + ((y - k)^{2})/(b^{2}) &= 1 \\\\\n((x - 1)^{2})/(5^{2}) + ((y - (-1))^{2})/(3^{2}) &= 1 \\\\\n((x - 1)^{2})/(25) + ((y + 1)^{2})/(9) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087467 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57e19ee3-db9a-58d7-8ca3-4e8801aee656" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Tall Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "e01914e2-b4f0-52b5-baf1-8b7fa5da3f18" :question/math-academy-id "e-5222" :question/problem "What is the equation of the ellipse given below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/849/Source/Images/0de79119b131777f87a4e2fdd6f3a262.png)" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{{a}^2}+\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{{b}^2}=1\n$$\n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-1,-3)$, the horizontal radius has length $a=2$, and the vertical radius has length $b=4$. Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((x - h)^{2})/(a^{2}) + ((y - k)^{2})/(b^{2}) &= 1 \\\\\n((x - (-1))^{2})/(2^{2}) + ((y - (-3))^{2})/(4^{2}) &= 1 \\\\\n((x + 1)^{2})/(4) + ((y + 3)^{2})/(16) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087468 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5028940-be34-56b7-8947-6129252f06a7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "10dda4fa-e047-53f4-a20a-b881e14f3e2a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5223" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\dfrac{(x-2)^2}{25} + \\dfrac{(y+2)^2}{16} = 1$, find its center, and the lengths of its major and minor axes." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{{a}^2}+\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{{b}^2}=1\n$$\n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=2$ and $k=-2$, so the center is located at $(2,-2)$.\n\nIn the given equation, we also have $a^2=25$ and $b^2=16$.\n\n- Since $a^2=25$, the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{25} = 5$.\n- Since $b^2=16$, the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{16} = 4$.\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=5$, and the semi-minor axis has length $b=4$.\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length\n\n$$\n2a = 2\\cdot5 = 10\n$$\n\nand the minor axis has length\n\n$$\n2b = 2\\cdot4 = 8\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "19d1448c-d5fd-5615-b657-ff4d66867834" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Wide Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "646c5754-af2a-5105-9ca0-45b6d897fa64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267507} {:answer/id #uuid "9f316800-04f0-5737-8506-96e5ac596714" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267508} {:answer/id #uuid "8843e2a2-e41a-584a-9188-05b9f27fd30a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267509} {:answer/id #uuid "0dc4c89d-07de-5ed8-a1db-6ab1171a43cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267510} {:answer/id #uuid "0baf5261-e94f-5a54-b879-c1d8cf85b463" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0baf5261-e94f-5a54-b879-c1d8cf85b463" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267511} :answer-field/id #uuid "e50d38e0-2af9-5e80-877d-f0e939caec99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267506}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3062a1ec-dc40-5c7d-b563-49ce6f30cc67" :question/math-academy-id "q-115286" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/assets/07003c76b7939be373eb4b1dc810e721f44b8c0405ee5312fd211f3b9959634b.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-4,3),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=4,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-4))^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57e19ee3-db9a-58d7-8ca3-4e8801aee656" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Tall Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed361dfa-6b16-5d9d-b7cb-81007146a4bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267514} {:answer/id #uuid "78c3f94c-c4a9-5bf9-80f4-462cd2099cda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267515} {:answer/id #uuid "236f14d7-96f1-5754-ada4-0b63acc9f922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267516} {:answer/id #uuid "18dbd1de-338c-50e0-a5b2-c658362e911e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267517} {:answer/id #uuid "e92be744-a538-5e8e-8575-cf982b7165c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{12}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267518}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18dbd1de-338c-50e0-a5b2-c658362e911e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267517} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4f66672-6bc0-5b5e-bf3b-cd857be4351f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267513}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "88a83a53-4b54-5fb6-8023-0cc5bd988bf8" :question/math-academy-id "q-115297" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/assets/1006eabe3321beb48df2cd936ff2252b77e8ded81f9c5b7aec4d143e43941088.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-4,0),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=2,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=6.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-4))^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-0)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267519 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5028940-be34-56b7-8947-6129252f06a7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adf73a4d-9982-5d4b-ac21-2c0249b86c04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$16$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267521} {:answer/id #uuid "6d28ada4-9e2a-50ac-9caa-04804b72e507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $1.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267522} {:answer/id #uuid "4e06cd29-e31f-5f19-b9c4-c1d3169194f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267523} {:answer/id #uuid "05712535-f147-51fc-aa8d-1781c6b2ab21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267524} {:answer/id #uuid "9c20f29b-dfe1-58eb-9af6-bc4727a6a082" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $2\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c20f29b-dfe1-58eb-9af6-bc4727a6a082" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $2\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267525} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f761cbe-abb1-559e-9aa7-659ab750727f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267520}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2986ed1-80e7-54b4-a24b-cb54615112bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-115312" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{3}=1$, find the lengths of its major and minor axes." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=4$ and $k=5,$ so the center is located at $(4,5).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=16$ and ${b}^{2}=3.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=16,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=3,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=4,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 4=8,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=2\\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267526 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5028940-be34-56b7-8947-6129252f06a7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68efc7e8-3441-55ff-b591-6c2ac13efc7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$25$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267528} {:answer/id #uuid "2e99faa3-8566-5a6f-a7f3-fd094689f60d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267529} {:answer/id #uuid "701f5a57-57a7-5b10-b7bf-8294bd1be08f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ and $2\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267530} {:answer/id #uuid "8266e82f-ea51-54aa-b30c-81394bcaa0a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267531} {:answer/id #uuid "600683ff-0fc4-57eb-be3a-95ef45e63783" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12.5$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "701f5a57-57a7-5b10-b7bf-8294bd1be08f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ and $2\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267530} :answer-field/id #uuid "68cf1a26-32b1-5ade-a1c6-5c839bc90d21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267527}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6821b43a-88ac-5b4b-9151-2f35f973a4c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-115313" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{6}=1$, find the lengths of its major and minor axes." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=-5$ and $k=2,$ so the center is located at $(-5,2).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=25$ and ${b}^{2}=6.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=25,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{25}=5.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=6,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{6}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=5,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\sqrt{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 5=10,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{6}=2\\sqrt{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5028940-be34-56b7-8947-6129252f06a7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa2581e0-79c6-519e-8744-4b424c92c926" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{5}$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267535} {:answer/id #uuid "9e2b8d5b-204d-547e-9b2f-1597a2a9dbe4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{5}$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267536} {:answer/id #uuid "ed738c7b-483b-56e8-a3b0-1c95058cfe3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267537} {:answer/id #uuid "fe487ffe-6654-55ee-9fa9-d3154382d03e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{5}$ and $2\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267538} {:answer/id #uuid "84162d1a-da76-5322-b679-5d9d147be955" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{5}$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe487ffe-6654-55ee-9fa9-d3154382d03e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{5}$ and $2\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267538} :answer-field/id #uuid "cce0e58f-a48b-56a7-87db-c9ef83a4c6b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267534}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8cb21ae0-f2f0-5250-97c3-5094275c61e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-16465" :question/problem "For the ellipse \n$\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{20}=1$, find the lengths of its major and minor axes." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=-1$ and $k=-3,$ so the center is located at $(-1,-3).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=5$ and ${b}^{2}=20.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=5,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{5}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=20,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{5},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{5}=4\\sqrt{5},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{5}=2\\sqrt{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57e19ee3-db9a-58d7-8ca3-4e8801aee656" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Tall Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34628de9-258f-5d53-909a-59d1d21e2165" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267542} {:answer/id #uuid "b581842c-4283-59cf-b828-367c0105818f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267543} {:answer/id #uuid "6df25aa2-352c-5495-8216-5c1dbfaa84a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267544} {:answer/id #uuid "846fb119-bb52-5c4d-83be-e8bee1e23474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267545} {:answer/id #uuid "47888f8c-586d-5488-99e1-8e5fc39b4493" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6df25aa2-352c-5495-8216-5c1dbfaa84a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267544} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef84a0d9-abca-59e6-9dfb-84329e3f1050" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267541}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b22ea02-8b75-5f49-8c84-897dbc1c2b11" :question/math-academy-id "q-16553" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/assets/0efd7cec177f27696620bba018055fb6c834823d3c13413b325b4d69095e07dd.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(4,-2),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=1,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-2))^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5028940-be34-56b7-8947-6129252f06a7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d078fd87-3cca-57a7-9be9-d4bfea6c0de9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d078fd87-3cca-57a7-9be9-d4bfea6c0de9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267549} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d44e0a4-d650-50e4-bb0f-f1f701607920" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267548} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a5e8a0e-126a-5e65-9f50-dc6f94589ea2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a5e8a0e-126a-5e65-9f50-dc6f94589ea2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267551} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa9a0b25-e291-5708-805c-2a55b3815790" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267550}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "411c201c-8678-5df1-8612-44d7e43ee539" :question/math-academy-id "q-277200" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=-4$ and $k=-3,$ so the center is located at $(-4,-3).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=36$ and ${b}^{2}=12.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=36,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{36}=6.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=12,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=6,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 6=12,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}=4\\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57e19ee3-db9a-58d7-8ca3-4e8801aee656" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Tall Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d972cb7-f674-5723-919a-f6d3dc0421ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+1)^2}{4}+\\frac{(y+3)^2}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d972cb7-f674-5723-919a-f6d3dc0421ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+1)^2}{4}+\\frac{(y+3)^2}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267554} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed274ac7-35bf-5a13-8527-60067f477907" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267553}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "01c8b954-6543-5e80-b060-0261735b6399" :question/math-academy-id "q-278254" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/assets/65a76a438b3459a92db0e00b0c9bd281b8a9c58fa68afd2d8e6468a1d99b1cf9.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-1,-3),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=2,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-1))^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-3))^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267555 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57e19ee3-db9a-58d7-8ca3-4e8801aee656" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Tall Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe076377-8319-5645-a6ba-da20eaf3197e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-4)^2}{4}+\\frac{(y+1)^2}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267557} {:answer/id #uuid "77f37205-cdd0-5c13-b381-5ff81058df31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-4)^2}{25}+\\frac{(y+1)^2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe076377-8319-5645-a6ba-da20eaf3197e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-4)^2}{4}+\\frac{(y+1)^2}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267557} :answer-field/id #uuid "86eeb558-0a17-5eec-92c7-1bda3882a012" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267556}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9bf6e24e-9e09-52c8-ac2f-212d781bbce7" :question/math-academy-id "q-278388" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/assets/8d6064ed62a5bdac5bac8c77e58617a5179d71ffe6cd0dc25f902af5e56bae07.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(4,-1),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=2,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=5.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-1))^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{25} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5028940-be34-56b7-8947-6129252f06a7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97a56b89-7939-55e5-aa7f-0de277930e6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97a56b89-7939-55e5-aa7f-0de277930e6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267561} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0a3e32c-7502-5d12-a165-e06a2e52dcdd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267560} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "375397a9-b308-5964-af77-9cc319b096b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "375397a9-b308-5964-af77-9cc319b096b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267563} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d3002bc-bbe1-5c58-b61e-5c7eeded7b0d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267562}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b74a983b-5b3e-5c81-aeef-49a17e380aae" :question/math-academy-id "q-278402" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x+7)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{11}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=-7$ and $k=-5,$ so the center is located at $(-7,-5).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=3$ and ${b}^{2}=11.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=3,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=11,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{11}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=\\sqrt{11},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{11}=2\\sqrt{11},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=2\\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267564 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "19d1448c-d5fd-5615-b657-ff4d66867834" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Wide Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b53e384-6f12-5953-be2a-b32dbf3bfe9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+1)^2}{16}+(y+3)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b53e384-6f12-5953-be2a-b32dbf3bfe9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+1)^2}{16}+(y+3)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267566} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1609a5d-58ad-56cf-9408-b4744ef31d4f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267565}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8d0ac665-608e-5cb2-bf77-2d2a2418aeac" :question/math-academy-id "q-278476" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/assets/8af601d0056c7ec678d6b21ba689413ec4b6f7107ab971394cf11316f5ec6f47.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-1,-3),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=4,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=1.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-1))^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-3))^{2}}{{1}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+(y+3)^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267567 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "19d1448c-d5fd-5615-b657-ff4d66867834" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Wide Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "541fe309-062a-5132-8b12-13fa70b5f480" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-1)^2}{16}+\\frac{(y+3)^2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "541fe309-062a-5132-8b12-13fa70b5f480" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-1)^2}{16}+\\frac{(y+3)^2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267569} :answer-field/id #uuid "53630e06-ce5e-5add-87e9-78e42312a628" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267568}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "61a1f7c0-e709-5866-b681-acddf66ca54c" :question/math-academy-id "q-278478" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/assets/591bf01f325ca1af8ffc34cb6c650cdf9024cf5dc672d9aed243723c476d7119.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(1,-3),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=4,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-3))^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267570 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "19d1448c-d5fd-5615-b657-ff4d66867834" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Wide Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bd084c2-a946-5ae9-9b58-a760dd2f274f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-3)^2}{16}+\\frac{y^2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267572} {:answer/id #uuid "6fdb1df1-d8af-5de3-8327-60e2ca39017e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+3)^2}{16}+\\frac{y^2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fdb1df1-d8af-5de3-8327-60e2ca39017e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+3)^2}{16}+\\frac{y^2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267573} :answer-field/id #uuid "17e39029-797b-5735-bfd0-ea075dc011e4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267571}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45384558-0ec9-5637-a95a-563522a4d678" :question/math-academy-id "q-278480" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/assets/c5672a0655ffd9ab3e098250e70f5bb782d14fd53c327ff7d14a1c0d798d25c4.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-3,0),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=4,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-3))^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-0)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267574 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "19d1448c-d5fd-5615-b657-ff4d66867834" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Wide Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76104ac7-9094-56d1-82ae-8f7e1ceb43b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{9}+(y-1)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267576} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c02dd7-eaa6-5e00-8c6e-7c8ad2010e69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{9}+(y+1)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267577} {:answer/id #uuid "f142485f-b766-5086-aef3-c28c956b1c14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3}+(y+1)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267578} {:answer/id #uuid "36c8a82d-2a7e-5e6c-ba35-bc030e499070" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267579} {:answer/id #uuid "dd9bb62a-bab1-53b2-9775-02fa37f07172" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{3}+(y-1)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267580}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76104ac7-9094-56d1-82ae-8f7e1ceb43b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{9}+(y-1)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267576} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1704bdb-be48-5336-91fa-0aaadd417ee4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267575}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ea348db3-c885-5078-8158-806415007f4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-70479" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/assets/69bcf48e0a8838dd981ae2b9727465016e74a321034cddd1f00f9384202feffb.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-1,1),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=3,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=1.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-1))^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{{1}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{9}+(y-1)^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267581 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57e19ee3-db9a-58d7-8ca3-4e8801aee656" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Tall Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62f3db3d-5735-5faf-81bf-cefe24de88fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267583} {:answer/id #uuid "43bd5ea6-0301-56e9-8c7c-a1b548a2ae67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{9}+(y+2)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267584} {:answer/id #uuid "9add08b1-8336-57b1-9572-81bf40396815" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267585} {:answer/id #uuid "b647881b-35ee-58f9-8155-a91a17254dd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267586} {:answer/id #uuid "28ca37b7-8d58-5047-bcf6-c186bfa39cb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{9}+(y-2)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62f3db3d-5735-5faf-81bf-cefe24de88fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267583} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8a692aa-2030-5107-8cc8-712b2f49e58d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267582}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe64cee3-670f-56ea-b466-db8bdf57c963" :question/math-academy-id "q-7619" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/assets/2bc4b82c6cfae63026f221dd49cdc74a9ce3884443e933cae02c36f6d732707e.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-2,4),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=1,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-2))^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
